{"type":"text","timestamp":1794377429927,"sessionID":"ses_e36c92cabffe1cdrQ9t4Hp4h3a","part":{"id":"prt_1c93ab38c001umGufTEjaOiDeU","messageID":"msg_1c93a6eab001dfBXJJ0UFCWXks","sessionID":"ses_e36c92cabffe1cdrQ9t4Hp4h3a","type":"text","text":"<think>Now the package-lock.json is updated. Let me verify `npm ci` works later.\n\nNow let me write the frontend. Plan of files:\n\nfrontend/\n  index.html (update title, add favicon maybe)\n  vite.config.ts (add tailwindcss plugin)\n  tsconfig.json (for typecheck)\n  src/\n    main.tsx\n    index.css\n    App.tsx\n    types.ts\n    api.ts\n    lib/format.ts\n    context/AuthContext.tsx\n    context/PlayerContext.tsx\n    components/AuthPanel.tsx\n    components/AddTrackForm.tsx\n    components/TrackList.tsx\n    components/PlayerBar.tsx\n\nLet me write the code.\n\n**types.ts**\n```ts\nexport type Track = {\n  id: number;\n  title: string;\n  artist: string;\n  url: string;\n  duration: number | string | null;\n  play_count: number;\n  created_at: string;\n  added_by: string | null;\n};\n\nexport type TrackPage = {\n  items: Track[];\n  total: number;\n  page: number;\n  page_size: number;\n};\n\nexport type User = { id: number; username: string };\n```\n\n**api.ts**\n```ts\nconst TOKEN_KEY = \"audio-library.token\";\n\nexport function getToken(): string | null\nexport function setToken(token: string | null): void\nexport class ApiError extends Error { status: number; errors: Record<string,string>; }\nexport async function apiRequest<T>(path: string, options: {method?, body?, auth?} = {}): Promise<T>\n```\n\n**AuthContext.tsx**\n- state: user | null, token\n- on mount: read token from localStorage; if present → GET /api/auth/me → set user or clear token.\n- login(username, password), register(username, password), logout(), loading state.\n\n**PlayerContext.tsx**\nProps: queue: Track[] (current page items), onPlayed?: (track: Track) => void (update play count in list).\n\nState: currentIndex (number), isPlaying, currentTime, duration (from audio metadata), volume.\n\nActually let me make the player own `currentTrack` and derive the index from the queue.\n\nKey logic:\n```tsx\nconst audioRef = useRef<HTMLAudioElement | null>(null);\nuseEffect(() => {\n  const audio = new Audio();\n  audio.preload = \"metadata\";\n  audioRef.current = audio;\n  const onTime = () => setCurrentTime(audio.currentTime);\n  const onMeta = () => setDuration(Number.isFinite(audio.duration) ? audio.duration : 0);\n  const onEnded = () => { next(); };\n  const onPlay = () => setIsPlaying(true);\n  const onPause = () => setIsPlaying(false);\n  ...\n  return () => { audio.pause(); audio.src = \"\"; };\n}, []);\n```\n\nplayTrack(track): find index in queue; set currentIndex; if the same track, toggle play.\n\nEffect on currentTrack change:\n```tsx\nuseEffect(() => {\n  const audio = audioRef.current;\n  if (!audio) return;\n  if (!currentTrack) { audio.removeAttribute(\"src\"); audio.load(); return; }\n  audio.src = currentTrack.url;\n  audio.volume = volumeRef.current;\n  const p = audio.play();\n  if (p) p.catch(() => setIsPlaying(false));\n  // increment play count\n  onPlayStart(currentTrack.id);\n}, [currentTrackId]);\n```\n\nCareful: `audio.play()` returns a promise that rejects if autoplay is blocked; that's fine (user gesture usually).\n\nThe play count increment: POST /api/tracks/{id}/plays → returns the track with the incremented play_count → call onPlayed(track) to update the list. Do it in the same effect when a new track starts (not when toggling pause/resume). Since the effect depends on currentTrackId only, it fires once per track change. But React StrictMode in dev double-invokes effects → double count in dev. I'll avoid StrictMode in main.tsx (or guard with a ref of last counted id). I'll use a ref to guard: `countedRef.current === id` → skip. Also not using StrictMode avoids double effects in dev. Let me not use StrictMode to keep the play count accurate. Hmm, StrictMode is good practice... but the double effect would cause double play() calls and double count. I'll include StrictMode but guard the counting with a ref. Double `audio.play()` is harmless. Actually in StrictMode, the mount effect creates the Audio element, cleanup pauses it and the second run creates another. That works fine.\n\nBut the guard ref: with StrictMode remount, the ref persists (refs aren't reset on the double-invoke of effects). The effect body runs twice with the same currentTrackId → second run sees countedRef === id → skips. Good.\n\nHmm, but a subtlety: StrictMode's double-mount in dev would also cause the \"currentTrack changed\" effect to run twice → the audio src set twice → fine.\n\nI'll include StrictMode with the ref guard.\n\nVolume: state in the provider; effect updates audio.volume.\n\nSeek: `audio.currentTime = t`.\n\nnext/previous: move index within the queue (wrap around). If the queue is empty, do nothing.\n\nAlso handle: when the current track is removed (deleted) → clear.\n\nI also want the player to expose `isCurrent(trackId)`.\n\n**App.tsx**\n- AuthProvider wraps everything; PlayerProvider needs the queue from App, but App is inside AuthProvider... Let me structure:\n\n```tsx\nexport default function App() {\n  return (\n    <AuthProvider>\n      <LibraryApp />\n    </AuthProvider>\n  );\n}\n```\n\nWhere LibraryApp holds tracks state and renders PlayerProvider with queue={tracks} and the rest.\n\n**TrackList**: table of tracks. Columns: # (play button), Title, Artist, Duration, Plays, Added by, Actions (delete).\n\n**AddTrackForm**: title, artist, url, duration inputs; on submit → POST /api/tracks; show API validation messages (field errors). On success → refresh the list (and maybe clear the form). Show a general error too.\n\n**Pagination**: page size selector (10/20/50), prev/next buttons, \"Page X of Y\".\n\n**Search box**: input with debounce → sets q and resets page to 1.\n\nLet me write the format helpers:\n- formatDuration(duration: number | string | null): string\n  - null → \"—\"\n  - number → m:ss (or h:mm:ss if >= 3600)\n  - string \"3:45\" → as is; \"210\" → parse as seconds → \"3:30\"\n- formatDurationSeconds(seconds: number): string\n\nFor the player seek bar, I need the track's duration in seconds: parse the duration value:\n```ts\nexport function durationToSeconds(duration: number | string | null): number | null\n```\n\nLet me now write all the files.\n\nAlso the index.html: title \"Audio Library\", meta description, and a dark background color to avoid flash (bg-neutral-950). I'll add `class=\"bg-zinc-950\"` on the body? With Tailwind v4, I can add `@layer base { body { @apply bg-zinc-950 text-zinc-100; } }` in index.css. Using @apply in v4 works. Or simply set the body class in index.html: `<body class=\"bg-zinc-950 text-zinc-100\">`. Tailwind v4 scans HTML files for classes too (it scans the whole project by default, excluding gitignored). index.html is in the root, so its classes are detected. I'll use @layer base in CSS for the body background to be safe, and also classes on the body element. Let me just do the CSS approach:\n\n```css\n@import \"tailwindcss\";\n\n@layer base {\n  html {\n    color-scheme: dark;\n  }\n  body {\n    @apply bg-zinc-950 text-zinc-100 antialiased;\n  }\n}\n```\n\nIn Tailwind v4, `@apply` inside `@layer base` works.\n\nAlso custom scrollbar? Skip.\n\nLet me write the files now.\n\nOne more: the player bar should show the current track's title and artist, play/pause, seek, volume, next/previous.\n\nLet me write the code.\n\nFor icons, I'll use inline SVG (no extra deps).\n\nLet me write api.ts first.\n\n```ts\nexport class ApiError extends Error {\n  status: number;\n  errors: Record<string, string>;\n  constructor(status: number, message: string, errors: Record<string,string> = {}) {\n    super(message);\n    this.status = status;\n    this.errors = errors;\n  }\n}\n\nasync function request<T>(path: string, init: RequestInit & { auth?: boolean } = {}): Promise<T> {\n  const headers: Record<string,string> = { Accept: \"application/json\" };\n  if (init.body !== undefined) headers[\"Content-Type\"] = \"application/json\";\n  const token = getToken();\n  if (init.auth !== false && token) headers[\"Authorization\"] = `Bearer ${token}`;\n  const res = await fetch(`/api${path}`, { ...init, headers });\n  ...\n}\n```\n\nNote: for the plays endpoint we can include the token if present (harmless).\n\nHandle non-JSON responses (e.g. 404 HTML) gracefully.\n\nNow writing all frontend files.</think>\n\nNow the frontend. Writing the API client and helpers first:","time":{"start":1794377429900,"end":1794377429921}}}
