{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794377650510,"sessionID":"ses_e36c92cabffe1cdrQ9t4Hp4h3a","part":{"id":"prt_1c93e1137001UamTfejpJ9hhID","messageID":"msg_1c93df58200179DcGm6HrwXkdy","sessionID":"ses_e36c92cabffe1cdrQ9t4Hp4h3a","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1794377650511,"sessionID":"ses_e36c92cabffe1cdrQ9t4Hp4h3a","part":{"id":"prt_1c93e113b001d6cDhpIOHERj27","messageID":"msg_1c93df58200179DcGm6HrwXkdy","sessionID":"ses_e36c92cabffe1cdrQ9t4Hp4h3a","type":"text","text":"<think>No browser available. So I'll verify the frontend logic by reasoning and by a static review. Let me at least do a server-side render sanity check? Not practical with Vite/React SPA.\n\nAlternative: I can write a small JSDOM-based test to render the React app and simulate interactions. jsdom is available via npm. That would catch runtime errors (e.g., a bad import, a hook misuse). Let me try: install jsdom as a dev dependency temporarily (not in package.json to avoid polluting the lock file... hmm, npm install would modify package.json/lock).\n\nI could install jsdom into a separate temp directory outside the frontend project and use NODE_PATH. Let me do that in /workspace/.run/jsdomtest with its own package.json. Then write a test that uses esbuild (bundled with vite) to bundle the app... complicated.\n\nSimpler: use Vite's SSR build? Or use vitest? Not installed.\n\nAlternative approach: install jsdom + use vite's `build --ssr`? Hmm.\n\nActually, a simpler approach to catch runtime errors: use `vite build` (done) plus a jsdom test that imports the built bundle. The built bundle is an ES module that mounts to #root — I can load it in jsdom with a script tag and check the rendered DOM. But the bundle uses modern features; jsdom should handle it. The API calls would fail (fetch not defined in jsdom by default — jsdom has no fetch). I could stub fetch.\n\nLet me try this: create /workspace/.run/jsdomtest/package.json with jsdom, then a script that:\n1. Creates a jsdom with the built index.html.\n2. Stubs global fetch to return canned API responses.\n3. Evaluates the built JS bundle in the jsdom context.\n4. Checks the DOM for expected elements (header, \"Audio Library\", sign-in form).\n5. Simulates registering via the form? That's harder but possible with dispatching events.\n\nReact 19's createRoot with act() warnings — should still work.\n\nThis is a decent investment to validate the frontend actually renders. Let me try it — the risk is spending a lot of time on jsdom quirks. Let me attempt it with a time limit.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: the most valuable checks are (1) it renders without crashing, (2) the auth form works, (3) the track list renders with data, (4) the player bar appears. A jsdom test could do all of this by stubbing fetch.\n\nLet me try. First install jsdom in a temp project.</think>\n\n","time":{"start":1794377650491,"end":1794377650507}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794377666171,"sessionID":"ses_e36c92cabffe1cdrQ9t4Hp4h3a","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_DbtJ1HVOAXJx5pfblV73U1rA","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"mkdir -p /workspace/.run/jsdomtest && cd /workspace/.run/jsdomtest && timeout 300 npm init -y >/dev/null 2>&1 && timeout 300 npm install --no-audit --no-fund jsdom 2>&1 | tail -3"},"output":"\nadded 28 packages in 15s\n","metadata":{"output":"\nadded 28 packages in 15s\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"mkdir -p /workspace/.run/jsdomtest && cd /workspace/.run/jsdomtest && timeout 300 npm init -y >/dev/null 2>&1 && timeout 300 npm install --no-audit --no-fund jsdom 2>&1 | tail -3","time":{"start":1794377650500,"end":1794377666148}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[]}},"id":"prt_1c93e113e0018eiPCWmuyIvz8i","sessionID":"ses_e36c92cabffe1cdrQ9t4Hp4h3a","messageID":"msg_1c93df58200179DcGm6HrwXkdy"}}
